<BaseLayout
  title="research — christopher joy"
  description="Research and writing by Christopher J. Joy on Southeast Asian labour markets, trade policy, governance, and development economics."
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <h1>Research &amp; Writing</h1>
  </div>
  <hr class="rule-accent" />

  <p>
    My research applies quantitative methods to Southeast Asia: labour markets and female
    economic participation, trade policy, and state capacity, with country work on Viet Nam,
    Indonesia, and Thailand. I work primarily with Stata, R, and Python.
  </p>

  <ul class="research-list">
    <li>
      <span class="research-tag">In Progress</span>
      <span class="research-title">Viet Nam and US Tariffs</span>
      <p class="research-desc">
        An analysis of shifting US trade policy and its effects on Vietnam's export-oriented
        economy. Examines sectoral exposure — electronics, textiles, footwear — labour market
        dynamics, and the geopolitics of supply-chain diversification. Relevant to investors
        and businesses assessing Vietnam's trade and investment environment under tariff
        uncertainty.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="research-tag">MA Dissertation</span>
      <span class="research-title">The Embedded State and Vietnam's COVID-19 Response</span>
      <p class="research-desc">
        Argues that the Vietnamese party-state's embedded governance structures marshalled
        political mobilization and community-level compliance to contain COVID-19 — retracing
        how state capacity, not just epidemiology, shaped outcomes. Provides a framework for
        understanding how Vietnam's governance model operates under pressure, with implications
        for regulatory and policy-risk assessment.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="research-tag">MSc Dissertation</span>
      <span class="research-title">Found Women: Economic Development and Female Empowerment in Southeast Asia</span>
      <p class="research-desc">
        Quantitative analysis of female labor force participation across ASEAN economies,
        examining how development trajectories and institutional factors shape gender outcomes.
        Southeast Asia's relative success on female economic inclusion — against the backdrop
        of the world's "missing women" — offers comparative insight for development and
        investment-environment assessment across the region.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="research-tag">Policy Brief</span>
      <span class="research-title">Nusantara: Ibukota Baru or Ghost Town?</span>
      <p class="research-desc">
        Assessment of Indonesia's capital relocation project: the political economy driving
        the decision, environmental and logistical risks, and the economic rationale — and
        limits — of moving the seat of government from Jakarta. Illustrative of the kind of
        regulatory and investment-environment analysis applied across the region.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="research-tag">Article</span>
      <span class="research-title">Private Equity, The Enabling State and The Logics of Infrastructure Investment</span>
      <p class="research-desc">
        Using England's water infrastructure as a case, argues that enabling states and
        privatisation created conditions for large private equity pools to rearrange asset
        logics in public infrastructure — a framework with relevance to infrastructure
        investment dynamics in emerging markets.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="research-tag">Policy Brief</span>
      <span class="research-title">Extra-welfarism: Welfare Economics and The Expanded Childhood Tax Credit</span>
      <p class="research-desc">
        Analysis of the US expanded Child Tax Credit under the American Rescue Plan Act of
        2021 through the lens of welfare economics and extra-welfarism — an exercise in
        translating policy design into evaluative economic frameworks.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
